\DOC new_coinductive_set

\TYPE {new_coinductive_set : term -> thm * thm * thm}

\SYNOPSIS
Define a set or family of sets coinductively.

\DESCRIBE
The function {new_coinductive_set} is applied to a conjunction of ``rules'',
each of the form {!x1...xn. Pi ==> ti IN Sk}, as for {new_inductive_set}, but
this conjunction is interpreted as a coinductive definition of a family of
sets {Sk}. That is, the sets are defined to be the largest ones consistent
with the rules, in the sense that each of their elements arises by one of the
rules from elements of the sets. The function {new_coinductive_set} will
convert this into explicit definitions, define a new constant for each {Sk},
and return a triple of theorems: the rule theorem, the coinduction theorem and
the cases theorem, as for {new_coinductive_definition}.

\FAILURE
Fails if the clauses are malformed, if the constants are already in use, or if
there are unproven monotonicity hypotheses. See {new_coinductive_definition}
for more detailed discussion in the similar case of coinductive relations.

\EXAMPLE
The set of points from which there is an infinite path in a relation {R}:
{
  # let DIVERGING_RULES,DIVERGING_COINDUCT,DIVERGING_CASES =
      new_coinductive_set
       `!x:A y. R x y /\ y IN diverging R ==> x IN diverging R`;;
  val DIVERGING_RULES : thm =
    |- !R x y. R x y /\ y IN diverging R ==> x IN diverging R
  val DIVERGING_COINDUCT : thm =
    |- !R diverging'.
           (!a. diverging' a ==> (?y. R a y /\ diverging' y))
           ==> (!a. diverging' a ==> a IN diverging R)
  val DIVERGING_CASES : thm =
    |- !R a. a IN diverging R <=> (?y. R a y /\ y IN diverging R)
}

\SEEALSO
new_coinductive_definition, new_inductive_set.

\ENDDOC
